# Sources: Fakhreddin Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, 1941–1953,
# pp. 207–208; Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5,
# pp. 250–267. Wider responsibility is deliberately left unassigned.
title: The <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> responds to Hazhir's assassination
subtitle: Political violence tests a coalition founded on constitutional method
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1949 and month >= 11 and hazhir_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 30
on-arrival: hazhir_seen = 1

= November 1949

{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Abdolhossein Hazhir{!</span>!} is
assassinated amid the election crisis from which the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}National Front{!</span>!} is
emerging. The killing is a fact. It does not establish that the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} planned,
approved, or knew of it, and the coalition does not possess evidence with
which to make that claim.

Silence, however, will also be interpreted. An unqualified condemnation may
reassure the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} but strain allies who want the election crisis kept in view.
A joint statement can address both matters but risks blurring them. Waiting
for better reports protects the evidentiary standard while leaving newspapers
and officials to interpret the delay.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} controls only its own response. What will it put into the public
record?

- @hazhir_condemn
- @hazhir_bounded
- @hazhir_verify

@hazhir_condemn
title: Issue an immediate, unqualified condemnation
subtitle: The <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> draws a bright line against political murder, even where allies want the election crisis named in the same breath
on-arrival: hazhir_response = "Unqualified condemnation"; constitutional_legitimacy += 4; religious_network_relation -= 1; shah_relation += 1

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s statement condemns the assassination and insists that political
change must proceed through lawful and representative institutions. It avoids
every unsupported claim about accomplices or motives.

{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} circles receive the clarity they demanded. Some religious-political
partners believe the statement gives too little attention to the coercion
surrounding the election, leaving the coalition more legitimate but slightly
less comfortable internally.

- @post_event: Continue

@hazhir_bounded
title: Publish a joint constitutional statement
subtitle: The statement condemns the killing while refusing to let the election crisis disappear behind it
on-arrival: hazhir_response = "Joint constitutional statement"; constitutional_legitimacy += 3; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 2; religious_network_dissent -= 1

The joint statement condemns the killing, then separates it from the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s
documented objections to election interference. Editors circulate only claims
the participating lawyers and politicians are prepared to defend.

The result is less absolute than a one-line condemnation but more useful to a
coalition that must discuss violence and electoral coercion without treating
them as the same allegation. Uncertainty about the killing remains explicit.

- @post_event: Continue

@hazhir_verify
title: Delay the full statement while reports are checked
subtitle: Silence protects the evidentiary standard and leaves newspapers and the <span class="term term-royalist">Court</span> to define the delay
on-arrival: hazhir_response = "Delayed verified statement"; constitutional_legitimacy += 2; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 2; press_capacity -= 1; shah_relation -= 1

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} issues only a brief condemnation while its contacts compare reports
about the killing and the response of the authorities. The pause prevents
unverified detail from entering the coalition's own account.

The cost is political: newspapers have little common language to print, and
the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} reads the delay as
reluctance. Checking the record does not remove the need for a constitutional
position.

- @post_event: Continue

# Source: Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5,
# pp. 250–267. Choice effects are counterfactual abstractions.
@tehran_rerun
title: Tehran's election is cancelled
subtitle: A second contest must produce both defensible candidates and defensible evidence
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1950 and month >= 1 and tehran_rerun_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 10
