on-arrival: {!
Q.chambers_open_seen = 1;
Q.majles_convened = 1;
Q.senate_convened = 1;
Q.majles_phase = "Credentials under review";
var vacantIndexes = [0, 87, 107, 126, 128];
var nfIndexes = [38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 44, 45, 105];
for (var i = 0; i < 136; i += 1) {
    if (vacantIndexes.indexOf(i) < 0) Q.majles_places[i].scenario.current_return = "returned";
}
// Historical evidence remains untouched. Each historically uncompleted place
// resolves separately from local organization, targeted work, legal defense,
// and Crown/administrative pressure.
for (var extraIndex of vacantIndexes) {
    var contestPlace = Q.majles_places[extraIndex];
    var contestMargin = contestPlace.scenario.player_influence +
        contestPlace.scenario.campaign_investment +
        contestPlace.scenario.legal_defense -
        contestPlace.scenario.administrative_pressure;
    if (contestMargin >= 45) {
        contestPlace.scenario.current_return = "returned";
        contestPlace.scenario.scenario_return_label =
            "Opposition-supported candidate (counterfactual)";
        contestPlace.scenario.support = "national_front";
        contestPlace.scenario.credential_contest = "pending";
    }
}
var openingCredentials = 0;
for (var c = 0; c < 136 && openingCredentials < 98; c += 1) {
    if (vacantIndexes.indexOf(c) < 0 && nfIndexes.indexOf(c) < 0) {
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.credential = "approved";
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.usability = "usable";
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.attendance = "attending";
        openingCredentials += 1;
    }
}
for (var n of nfIndexes) {
    Q.majles_places[n].scenario.support = "national_front";
}
for (var s = 0; s < 60; s += 1) {
    Q.senate_places[s].scenario.current_return = "returned";
    Q.senate_places[s].scenario.credential = "approved";
    Q.senate_places[s].scenario.usability = "usable";
    Q.senate_places[s].scenario.attendance = "attending";
}
!}

= February 1950

[? if election_strategy = "Concentrated Tehran slate": The concentrated slate
has made the returned Tehran group politically legible, but its legal files
are uneven. ?]
[? if election_strategy = "Monitoring and credential record": The monitoring
strategy enters the chamber with stronger files and a less concentrated
electoral identity. ?]
[? if election_strategy = "Slate with selective monitoring": The mixed
strategy has produced both candidates and files without fully funding either
outside the selected contests. ?]

The Sixteenth {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} and the
first {!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!} open. The {!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!}
combines elected and royally appointed routes. In the {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}, the opening
proceedings record 98 approved credentials, with other decisions still to
come.

That procedure matters. A constituency may have returned a name, but a deputy
cannot act as a usable member until the chamber accepts the credential and the
member attends. Nor does an approved credential establish a permanent party
alignment. The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s small group must build capacity inside a chamber that
cannot be reduced to a stable government and opposition chart.

The first parliamentary priority will shape how those deputies work while the
remaining credentials are reviewed.

- @opening_procedure
- @opening_coalition
- @opening_liaison

@opening_procedure
title: Prepare case-by-case credential objections
subtitle: Each return is tested on its own record, while coalition strategy develops more slowly
on-arrival: credentials_strategy = "Case-by-case procedural challenge"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 4

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s deputies and lawyers organize each objection around a particular
return, credential rule, and supporting record. They avoid pretending that one
national slogan can establish every local case.

The method strengthens their standing when procedure is contested. It also
consumes the small group's time one case at a time, leaving less attention for
a common parliamentary program.

- @post_event: Continue

@opening_coalition
title: Establish a coordinated <span class="term term-national-front">National Front</span> group
subtitle: The returned deputies speak as one group, even when they have not mastered every procedural weapon
on-arrival: credentials_strategy = "Coordinated National Front group"; independent_nationalists_relation += 3; oil_coalition_support += 2

The deputies establish a regular meeting, divide responsibility for following
debates, and agree to consult before questions affecting the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s common
program. {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Independent nationalists{!</span>!} acquire a parliamentary routine that no
single party can impose.

Coordination makes the small delegation more coherent. It does not give it a
majority, settle disputed credentials, or guarantee that every ally will vote
the same way.

- @post_event: Continue

@opening_liaison
title: Assign deputies to cultivate procedural allies
subtitle: The <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> trades some internal discipline for allies who can move a chamber it does not control
on-arrival: credentials_strategy = "Deputy liaison and procedural outreach"; oil_coalition_support += 5; independent_nationalists_dissent += 1

Individual {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies begin regular conversations with members outside the
group about scheduling, credentials, and the coming oil question. The approach
does not label those members as permanent allies; it identifies issues on
which cooperation may be possible.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} learns more about the chamber and widens its reach. Its own group
meets less often, and some members worry that private understandings will
substitute for a common line.

- @post_event: Continue

# Source: official Sixteenth-Majles proceedings, sessions 25 (18 May 1950)
# and 30 (1 June 1950). Choice effects are counterfactual abstractions.
@credential_campaign
title: The credential campaign
subtitle: Chamber decisions make the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span>'s returned deputies usable in successive waves
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1950 and month >= 5 and credentials_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 10
on-arrival: {!
Q.credentials_seen = 1;
var nfIndexes = [38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 44, 45, 105];
for (var n of nfIndexes) {
    Q.majles_places[n].scenario.current_return = "returned";
    Q.majles_places[n].scenario.credential = "approved";
    Q.majles_places[n].scenario.usability = "usable";
    Q.majles_places[n].scenario.attendance = "attending";
    Q.majles_places[n].scenario.support = "national_front";
}
var approvedCount = 0;
for (var place of Q.majles_places) {
    if (place.scenario.credential === "approved") approvedCount += 1;
}
for (var c = 0; c < 136 && approvedCount < 120; c += 1) {
    if (Q.majles_places[c].scenario.current_return === "returned" &&
        !Q.majles_places[c].scenario.scenario_return_label &&
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.credential !== "approved") {
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.credential = "approved";
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.usability = "usable";
        Q.majles_places[c].scenario.attendance = "attending";
        approvedCount += 1;
    }
}
var counterfactualUsable = 0;
for (var counterfactualPlace of Q.majles_places) {
    if (counterfactualPlace.scenario.scenario_return_label) {
        var credentialMargin =
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.player_influence +
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.legal_defense -
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.administrative_pressure;
        if (credentialMargin >= 55) {
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.credential = "approved";
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.usability = "usable";
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.attendance = "attending";
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.credential_contest = "won";
            counterfactualUsable += 1;
        } else {
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.credential = "rejected";
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.usability = "unavailable";
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.attendance = "absent";
            counterfactualPlace.scenario.credential_contest = "lost";
        }
    }
}
Q.oil_coalition_support += counterfactualUsable * 2;
!}

= May–June 1950

[? if credentials_strategy = "Case-by-case procedural challenge": The
parliamentary group has prepared to argue each credential from its own
record. ?]
[? if credentials_strategy = "Coordinated National Front group": The returned
deputies approach the decisions as a visible group, even where their files
differ in strength. ?]
[? if credentials_strategy = "Deputy liaison and procedural outreach":
Private liaison has created allies in the chamber, but not a single public
account of the disputed returns. ?]

Credential decisions arrive as successive acts of the chamber, not as one
national result. The official proceedings record approval of
{!<span class="term term-constitutionalist">!}Allahyar Saleh{!</span>!}'s
Kashan–Natanz credential on 18 May and the eleven Tehran credentials on
1 June. Those decisions help make the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s returned group usable.

A credential fight is both legal and political. Publishing a well-supported
case can make procedure visible outside the chamber, but public pressure can
make later negotiation harder. Quiet scheduling and deputy outreach can
secure attention for petitions without building the same public record.

[? if credentials_strategy = "Case-by-case procedural challenge": Existing
files make the published route more credible, but they have not assembled a
floor coalition. ?]
[? if credentials_strategy = "Coordinated National Front group": The regular
deputy meeting can coordinate interventions, though some cases remain stronger
than others. ?]
[? if credentials_strategy = "Deputy liaison and procedural outreach":
Private allies can help schedule petitions, but they do not supply the same
public constitutional record. ?]

- @credentials_public
- @credentials_internal
- @credentials_combined

@credentials_public
title: Publish the strongest documented credential cases
subtitle: Only the best cases go before the country, making royal interference part of the public dispute
on-arrival: credential_campaign_response = "Published credential cases"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 5; press_capacity += 2; shah_electoral_influence -= 2

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} publishes only cases for which its lawyers can identify the return,
the objection, and the evidence being presented to the chamber. Readers can
see why approval of one credential does not automatically settle another.

The campaign makes interference more costly to defend in public. It also
hardens the contest with networks accustomed to exercising electoral
influence through less visible channels.

- @post_event: Continue

@credentials_internal
title: Negotiate scheduling and support inside the chamber
subtitle: Quiet scheduling and persuasion win room inside the chamber without creating a public cause
on-arrival: credential_campaign_response = "Internal chamber negotiation"; oil_coalition_support += 5; independent_nationalists_relation += 3

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies meet colleagues case by case, asking when a petition will be
heard and which procedural claim a member is willing to support. The
conversations also reveal who may later listen on oil even if they will not
join the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}.

The approach preserves working relationships and broadens support. It does
less to explain the credential struggle to people outside the
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}.

- @post_event: Continue

@credentials_combined
title: Pair selected petitions with a public legal brief
subtitle: Two resources join selected petitions to a public legal brief, dividing effort between chamber and country
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} needs 2 resources for legal preparation and printing.
on-arrival: credential_campaign_response = "Petitions and public brief"; resources -= 2; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 4; press_capacity += 1; oil_coalition_support += 3

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} chooses a small number of petitions for full legal preparation and
prints a brief that explains their procedure without declaring every other
case fraudulent. Deputies can negotiate around a document they are prepared
to defend.

The combined effort is expensive and selective. It creates neither a complete
national audit nor a permanent parliamentary alliance, but it links public
explanation to work inside the chamber.

- @post_event: Continue

# Sources: David S. Painter and Gregory Brew, The Struggle for Iran, ch. 1;
# Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5, pp. 250–267;
# official Special Oil Commission minutes, first period, meeting 1.
@oil_committee_formation
title: The oil committee
subtitle: A small <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> delegation gains a forum for detailed scrutiny
new-page: true
