= Mid-1950

The {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} creates an
eighteen-member special oil committee to consider the supplemental agreement.
Five members belong to the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}National Front{!</span>!}. The
proportion gives a small parliamentary delegation an important forum, not
control over the committee or the chamber.

[? if credential_campaign_response = "Published credential cases": The
published cases have made parliamentary procedure part of the wider
constitutional campaign. ?]
[? if credential_campaign_response = "Internal chamber negotiation": The
group enters the committee with stronger private contacts and a thinner public
record. ?]
[? if credential_campaign_response = "Petitions and public brief": Selected
legal cases now connect public explanation to deputy work, at substantial
cost. ?]

Committee work can turn a broad oil demand into a record: which terms alter
revenue, which leave ownership or operational control untouched, and which
questions remain unanswered. It can also identify deputies outside the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}
who may agree on a particular conclusion without joining its wider program.

What product should the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} prioritize in the committee's first period?

- @committee_terms
- @committee_majority
- @committee_public_brief

@committee_terms
title: Build a term-by-term committee record
subtitle: Each disputed clause becomes a question the agreement's defenders must answer; coalition courtship waits
on-arrival: oil_committee_response = "Term-by-term committee record"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 6; oil_coalition_support += 2; oil_terms_position = "Documented term-by-term rejection"

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} members organize the proposal by ownership recognition, operational
control, marketing, compensation, finance, personnel authority, and duration.
Where the record does not establish a term, the file says “unknown” rather
than supplying a convenient midpoint.

The matrix gives the committee a precise basis for scrutiny. Preparing it
takes time that might otherwise be spent asking additional deputies to commit
to a successor policy.

- @post_event: Continue

@committee_majority
title: Recruit support beyond the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span>'s five members
subtitle: The five <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> members look outward for votes before agreeing on every disputed term
on-arrival: oil_committee_response = "Cross-faction committee majority"; oil_coalition_support += 6; independent_nationalists_relation += 2; religious_network_relation += 2

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies meet committee colleagues around propositions they can state
without demanding membership in the coalition. The conversations search for a
majority on the agreement and on what should follow it.

The outreach widens support and gives independent and religious-political
partners a visible role. The resulting common position is broader than the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} and less detailed than a complete term record.

- @post_event: Continue

@committee_public_brief
title: Prepare a public brief from verified committee material
subtitle: Verified committee material leaves the chamber as a public case, inviting the <span class="term term-royalist">Court</span> to answer
on-arrival: oil_committee_response = "Public committee brief"; press_capacity += 4; public_mandate += 3; shah_resistance += 1

Editors work with the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s committee members to explain what the body is
examining and what it has not yet decided. The brief does not attribute a
statement to the committee unless it appears in the record.

The public gains a clearer view of an otherwise technical dispute. Moving the
argument outside the chamber also convinces the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} that committee procedure
is becoming a national political campaign.

- @post_event: Continue

# Sources: David S. Painter and Gregory Brew, The Struggle for Iran, ch. 1;
# Mostafa Elm, Oil, Power, and Principle, ch. 1. Choice effects are
# counterfactual abstractions.
@supplemental_campaign
title: The supplemental-agreement campaign
subtitle: More revenue does not by itself settle ownership, control, or duration
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1950 and month >= 8 and supplemental_campaign_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
