= August 1950

Debate over the Gass–Golshayan supplemental agreement intensifies. The proposal
would increase Iran's revenue while leaving the basic
{!<span class="term term-aioc">!}Anglo-Iranian Oil Company{!</span>!}
structure and operational control substantially intact. The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s objection
therefore concerns the distribution of authority as well as the amount paid.

Public comparisons with other oil arrangements can sharpen pressure, but
unlike contracts are not mechanically identical. Unknown or disputed terms
remain unknown.

[? if oil_committee_response = "Term-by-term committee record": The first
committee period has already given editors a clause-by-clause case. ?]
[? if oil_committee_response = "Cross-faction committee majority": Earlier
work concentrated on deputies who might support a conclusion without joining
the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}. ?]
[? if oil_committee_response = "Public committee brief": The committee
argument is already circulating outside the chamber, sharpening both mandate
and royal resistance. ?]

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} can translate this structured case through different political
channels. Each reaches a different audience and creates a different risk.

- @supplemental_explain
- @supplemental_mandate
- @supplemental_deputies

@supplemental_explain
title: Publish a term-by-term press dossier
subtitle: The dossier asks readers to judge the agreement clause by clause rather than by promised revenue
on-arrival: supplemental_response = "Term-by-term press dossier"; press_capacity += 5; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 2; constitutional_legitimacy += 2

The dossier separates additional revenue from ownership recognition,
operational control, marketing authority, personnel, compensation, interim
finance, and duration. Blank cells are described as unresolved rather than
quietly treated as acceptable.

Editors can now explain why the dispute is not reducible to one royalty
number. The precision strengthens the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s case, though it is slower to
carry into a mass meeting than a single slogan.

- @post_event: Continue

@supplemental_mandate
title: Organize public meetings around control and sovereignty
subtitle: Meetings turn operational control into a national demand—and a direct challenge to the <span class="term term-royalist">Crown</span>
on-arrival: supplemental_response = "Public meetings on sovereignty"; public_mandate += 5; oil_coalition_support += 3; shah_resistance += 2

Speakers focus on the question the supplemental agreement leaves open: who
will control the industry and on whose authority. They use the revenue
comparison as context without claiming that unlike contracts contain the same
terms.

The meetings widen the nationalization demand and bring new partners into the
oil coalition. The {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} increasingly sees the campaign as a challenge that
cannot be confined to committee procedure.

- @post_event: Continue

@supplemental_deputies
title: Give undecided deputies a private brief
subtitle: Undecided deputies receive the complete comparison in private, while the public campaign waits
on-arrival: supplemental_response = "Briefed undecided deputies"; oil_coalition_support += 6; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 3

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies circulate a short brief that states both its conclusion and its
limits. It distinguishes the agreement's revenue changes from the ownership
and control questions that remain, and it labels unresolved terms rather than
silently treating them as concessions.

The careful briefing gives wavering members a defensible reason to reject the
agreement. It does not build the same visible public mandate as meetings or a
press campaign.

- @post_event: Continue

