@committee_rejection
title: The oil committee rejects Gass–Golshayan
subtitle: The committee decision opens the question of what should replace the agreement
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1950 and month >= 11 and gass_golshayan_rejected_committee = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 10
on-arrival: gass_golshayan_rejected_committee = 1

= 25 November 1950

After its first period of work, the oil committee rejects the supplemental
agreement as an inadequate basis for securing Iran's rights. The decision is
an act of a {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} committee; it does not itself enact nationalization or
commit every deputy to the next measure.

For the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}, rejection changes the problem. It must carry the committee's
reasoning into the chamber and make clear what policy should follow, while
keeping the institutional victory from dissolving into a claim that the final
work is already done.

[? if supplemental_response = "Term-by-term press dossier": The press dossier
has made the reasons for rejection intelligible beyond the committee. ?]
[? if supplemental_response = "Public meetings on sovereignty": Public
meetings have made rejection a popular demand and have also hardened
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Crown{!</span>!} resistance. ?]
[? if supplemental_response = "Briefed undecided deputies": Quiet work with
undecided deputies has broadened the chamber coalition without producing the
same public campaign. ?]

- @committee_rejection_response

@committee_rejection_response
title: Decide how to carry the committee report forward
subtitle: The <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> must choose its next political instrument

- @committee_claim
- @committee_bridge
- @committee_joint

@committee_claim
title: Publish the committee reasoning as a constitutional report
subtitle: The committee's reasoning becomes the constitutional foundation for what follows, though the appeal remains narrow
on-arrival: committee_rejection_strategy = "Constitutional committee report"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 5; constitutional_legitimacy += 2

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} presents the rejection as the result of committee scrutiny, not as
an instruction issued by the street. Its report distinguishes the proposal
that has failed from the nationalization principle that has not yet passed.

The narrow claim is difficult to dismiss as procedural theater. It gives
potential allies less space, however, to join through a broader political
declaration.

- @post_event: Continue

@committee_bridge
title: Convene deputies around a successor nationalization motion
subtitle: A successor motion gives unlike deputies something affirmative to support before every term is settled
on-arrival: committee_rejection_strategy = "Successor nationalization motion"; oil_coalition_support += 6; public_mandate += 2

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} members use the rejection to ask deputies a new and concrete question:
whether the nationalization principle should replace another attempt to amend
the existing arrangement. Participation does not require joining the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}.

The meetings widen the possible majority. Because attention moves quickly to
the successor motion, fewer people learn the exact committee reasoning that
made rejection possible.

- @post_event: Continue

@committee_joint
title: Issue a joint parliamentary and public declaration
subtitle: Chamber argument and public declaration move together, making the <span class="term term-royalist">Court</span> part of the dispute
on-arrival: committee_rejection_strategy = "Joint declaration"; oil_coalition_support += 3; public_mandate += 3; press_capacity += 2; shah_resistance += 1

Deputies and coalition organizations release a common declaration: the
committee decision is documented, nationalization is the proposed next
principle, and implementation terms remain to be written later.

The declaration connects chamber work to public organization without claiming
that one commands the other. Its breadth also makes the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} more conscious
of a coordinated challenge extending beyond the {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}.

- @post_event: Continue

