= 11 January 1951

[? if dues_received_this_month > 0: Annual subscriptions have brought
[+ dues_received_this_month +] resources into the common treasury. ?]

The {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} follows the
committee and rejects the Gass–Golshayan supplemental agreement. The chamber
has refused this modification of the existing oil relationship. It has not
yet approved the nationalization principle or settled the terms that would
make that principle operational.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} must keep those stages distinct. It can make the parliamentary
record its foundation, gather deputies around the next measure, or explain to
the public why a rejected agreement is not yet a completed oil settlement.

[? if committee_rejection_strategy = "Constitutional committee report": The
commission's reasoning is already available as the constitutional basis for a
successor motion. ?]
[? if committee_rejection_strategy = "Successor nationalization motion": A
cross-faction group has begun turning rejection into an affirmative
principle. ?]
[? if committee_rejection_strategy = "Joint declaration": Parliament and the
public received the same declaration, leaving less room to retreat from its
language. ?]

- @majles_rejection_response

@majles_rejection_response
title: Turn rejection into preparation for the next vote
subtitle: Choose the institution and audience that should carry the argument

- @majles_record
- @majles_coalition
- @majles_movement

@majles_record
title: Issue a report on what the <span class="term term-parliament">Majles</span> did—and did not—decide
subtitle: The report denies the government a victory without pretending rejection has already enacted nationalization
on-arrival: majles_rejection_strategy = "Procedural report"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 4; constitutional_legitimacy += 3

The report describes the chamber's rejection without inventing a numerical
division or treating every supporting deputy as a permanent {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} member. It
then lists the questions a nationalization measure will still have to face.

The constitutional sequence becomes harder to blur. The report is
institutionally precise and less effective as a broad mobilizing message.

- @post_event: Continue

@majles_coalition
title: Convene a cross-faction meeting on the nationalization principle
subtitle: A private meeting tests who will cross from rejecting the agreement to endorsing the principle
on-arrival: majles_rejection_strategy = "Cross-faction nationalization meeting"; oil_coalition_support += 6; independent_nationalists_relation += 3

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies invite members who supported rejection to discuss the narrower
question of nationalization. They do not ask those deputies to accept the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s full program or assign them a permanent caucus label.

The meeting reveals where a majority may exist and which concerns remain
unanswered. Because it occurs inside the parliamentary world, its public
effect is limited.

- @post_event: Continue

@majles_movement
title: Organize a public explanation of the unfinished victory
subtitle: The unfinished victory is explained outside the chamber, where public expectations may outrun deputy commitments
on-arrival: majles_rejection_strategy = "Public unfinished-victory campaign"; oil_coalition_support += 3; public_mandate += 4; press_capacity += 2

Newspapers and meetings repeat a disciplined distinction: the supplemental
agreement has been rejected, but nationalization and its later implementation
have not yet been enacted. The public is asked to sustain pressure through the
next institutional stage.

The campaign grows without pretending the legal work is complete. More of the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s attention now sits outside the chamber as the next vote approaches.

- @post_event: Continue

