on-arrival: razmara_confrontation_seen = 1

= February 1951

{!<span class="term term-independent">!}Prime Minister Haj Ali
Razmara{!</span>!} argues that immediate nationalization carries serious
administrative and economic dangers. He presses the opposition on whether the
state has the trained personnel and institutional capacity to carry the
principle into operation.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}National Front{!</span>!} has
spent months arguing that additional revenue without control is inadequate.
It must now show whether nationalization is only a principle of ownership or a
position capable of answering implementation concerns—without pretending
that the later implementation law has already been written.

[? if majles_rejection_strategy = "Procedural report": The
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} record makes
it harder for the government to present rejection as acceptance of the old
concessionary structure. ?]
[? if majles_rejection_strategy = "Cross-faction nationalization meeting":
Deputies beyond the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}
have already been asked to support an affirmative
principle. ?]
[? if majles_rejection_strategy = "Public unfinished-victory campaign": The
public campaign is pressing the chamber to move beyond rejection. ?]

Who should carry the answer, and through which institution?

- @razmara_terms
- @razmara_public
- @razmara_joint

@razmara_terms
title: Answer through a term-by-term parliamentary brief
subtitle: Every administrative objection receives a parliamentary answer, keeping the contest inside the record
on-arrival: razmara_response = "Parliamentary term-by-term rebuttal"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 5; oil_coalition_support += 4

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies separate the nationalization principle from the operational
questions that must follow it. The brief identifies what is known, what
requires later legislation, and why those unknowns do not automatically
validate the supplemental agreement.

The response reassures deputies who want a procedural answer rather than a
slogan. It does not produce an immediate mass campaign, and several practical
questions remain genuinely open.

- @post_event: Continue

@razmara_public
title: Publish a public dossier on control and capacity
subtitle: The technical dispute becomes a public argument over who is fit to control the industry
on-arrival: razmara_response = "Public nationalization campaign"; public_mandate += 5; press_capacity += 3; shah_resistance += 2

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}'s editors explain the difference between a warning that
implementation will be difficult and a conclusion that Iran cannot claim
control. They acknowledge unresolved questions rather than assigning them
imaginary neutral terms.

The dossier carries the argument beyond the {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} and increases public
confidence in the campaign. The {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} reads the widening mobilization as a
direct challenge during an already tense confrontation.

- @post_event: Continue

@razmara_joint
title: Form a joint deputy-and-expert response group
subtitle: Two resources put deputies and specialists behind one answer for chamber and country
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} needs 2 resources for research, coordination, and printing.
on-arrival: resources -= 2; razmara_response = "Joint parliamentary and public response"; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 2; oil_coalition_support += 4; press_capacity += 2; public_mandate += 2

Deputies, lawyers, and technically informed supporters prepare one answer for
the chamber and a shorter version for public circulation. Both distinguish
the principle before the {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} from the implementation machinery that would
have to come later.

The coordinated response is expensive and cannot settle every operational
question. It keeps the parliamentary and public arguments from contradicting
one another.

- @post_event: Continue

