= 20 March 1951

The nationalization principle has passed the
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}. Senators must now
decide whether to complete the constitutional sequence. The opposition's
parliamentary record, oil coalition, relationship with the
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Crown{!</span>!}, and the pressure
surrounding the chamber determine whether that support holds.

Passage in the lower chamber does not supply an automatic upper-house
majority. Senators who have not made a firm commitment can still decide the
outcome.

[? if senate_strategy = "Institutional record": Senators receive the
commission and lower-house record as a deliberately staged constitutional
sequence. ?]
[? if senate_strategy = "Public briefings": The public case is at its widest,
while direct upper-house coordination has received fewer organizers. ?]
[? if senate_strategy = "Cross-chamber constitutional case": Deputies have
concentrated their remaining effort on an upper-house constitutional
majority. ?]

- @senate_vote_resolution: Call the vote

@senate_vote_resolution
title: Resolve the <span class="term term-senate">Senate</span> vote
subtitle: The senators expected to attend now decide whether to complete the constitutional sequence
on-arrival: {!
Q.nationalization_senate_vote_resolved = 1;
if (Q.nationalization_senate_support >= Q.nationalization_senate_threshold) {
    Q.nationalization_senate_passed = 1;
    Q.nationalization_approved_senate = 1;
    Q.majles_phase = "Nationalization approved by both chambers";
} else {
    Q.nationalization_senate_passed = 0;
    Q.majles_phase = "Nationalization blocked in the Senate";
    Q.terminal_reason = "Senate rejection";
    Q.ending_name = "Nationalization Blocked in the Senate";
    Q.campaign_complete = 1;
}
!}
go-to: senate_passage if nationalization_senate_passed = 1; senate_nationalization_defeat if nationalization_senate_passed = 0

@senate_passage
title: The <span class="term term-senate">Senate</span> approves nationalization
new-page: true

= 20 March 1951

The decision completes the constitutional sequence. It does not enact the
later implementation law or settle administration,
personnel, marketing, compensation, and interim finance. Those struggles lie
beyond this ending.

Of [+ senate_attending_members +] senators expected to attend, [+
nationalization_senate_support +] supported the principle, [+
nationalization_senate_conditional +] withheld a firm commitment, and [+
nationalization_senate_opposed +] opposed it. The majority required [+
nationalization_senate_threshold +].

The vote leaves behind a political organization, parliamentary position,
public network, and relationship with the
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Crown{!</span>!}.

- @finish_campaign

@senate_nationalization_defeat
title: The <span class="term term-senate">Senate</span> blocks nationalization
new-page: true

= 20 March 1951

The upper chamber does not supply the support required to complete the
nationalization principle. The {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}
vote remains a major political declaration, but it has not cleared both
chambers.

Of [+ senate_attending_members +] senators expected to attend, [+
nationalization_senate_support +] support the principle, [+
nationalization_senate_conditional +] withhold a firm commitment, and [+
nationalization_senate_opposed +] oppose it. The required majority is [+
nationalization_senate_threshold +].

This is a counterfactual result. The documented historical
{!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!} approved the principle;
this campaign ends with the coalition unable to reproduce that constitutional
sequence.

- @campaign_ending: Evaluate the campaign

@finish_campaign
title: Evaluate the campaign
subtitle: See the coalition and institutions produced by twenty-seven months of decisions
on-arrival: campaign_complete = 1

Nationalization has passed as a constitutional principle. The
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} carries forward the structure it built, the allies it relied on,
its parliamentary position, and its public organization.

- @post_event: Continue to the campaign evaluation
